              "text": "- **Dynamic, but strongly typed:** Julia checks types at runtime, not compile time in the way static languages do. You don’t declare variable types in advance, but values always have types, and operations are type-checked.\n\n- **Multiple dispatch is central:** Functions in Julia choose which method to run based on the types of *all* their arguments, not just one. This makes Julia’s type system tightly linked to how you write and optimize code.\n\n- **Types are used for performance and structure:** You can write generic code with abstract types and make it fast by specializing on concrete types. This lets Julia stay flexible like a dynamic language while still achieving near C-like performance in many cases."
            }
          ],
